   "captions_sha256": "131c28437e3cfd197228d1940378bbbd254aa95fe70d311c6f1bf943779a05bc",
   "created_at": "2026-11-19T07:44:43Z",
   "description": "NASA satellites watched a long red stripe of warm water spread across the tropical Pacific in 2026. It's El Niño. Then, on the other side of that ocean, snow fell on the Atacama Desert and the desert began to bloom.\n\nIn this episode:\n0:00 The red stripe that wasn't there a year ago\n0:23 How El Niño moves warm water east\n1:00 The ocean surface rises\n1:16 NASA's PACE satellite sees the ocean change color\n1:59 Rain and snow in the Atacama\n2:57 The flowering desert\n3:47 What comes next\n\nSources:\n- NASA Earth Observatory, \"El Niño Rains Paint the Atacama\" (Nov 19, 2026): https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/el-nino-rains-paint-the-atacama/\n- NASA Earth Observatory, \"El Niño Alters Marine Life in the Pacific\" (Aug 10, 2026): https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/el-nino-alters-marine-life-in-the-pacific/\n- NASA Earth Observatory, \"Rare, Widespread Snow in the Atacama Desert\" (Aug 27, 2026): https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/rare-widespread-snow-in-the-atacama-desert/\n- NASA Scientific Visualization Studio: https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/5683 , https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/5688 , https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/5687\n\nImagery: NASA Scientific Visualization Studio; NASA Earth Observatory (Michala Garrison, Lauren Dauphin) using Landsat data from the U.S. Geological Survey, MODIS (Terra) and PACE data. Public domain; NASA does not endorse this video.\nNarration: synthetic voice (ElevenLabs).\n\n#ElNino #NASA #Atacama",
